      <h1>Why Is My Paycheck So Low? Why Gross vs. Net Pay Has Such a Big Gap</h1>
      <div class="article-meta"><span>Sep 24, 2026</span><span>·</span><span>6 min read</span></div>
      <p class="article-intro">You signed an offer for $75,000 a year, but when your first paycheck lands in your account, it's only $2,100 instead of $2,884. Where did the other $784 go? Here is why your paycheck is so much lower than expected.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="container" style="max-width:740px;">
    <div class="prose">
      <div class="callout"><strong>The Golden Rule of Paychecks:</strong> On average, US workers take home only <strong>70% to 78%</strong> of their gross pay. The remaining 22% to 30% disappears into mandatory government taxes (Federal, Social Security, Medicare, State) and employer pre-tax benefits (401k, health insurance).</div>

      <h2>1. The Four Mandatory Payroll Taxes</h2>
      <p>Regardless of what state you live in, every W-2 employee in the United States is subject to mandatory federal payroll withholdings:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Federal Income Tax Withholding (10% to 37%):</strong> Based on your W-4 form, marital status, and IRS tax brackets. For single filers earning median wages, federal withholding usually claims 10% to 15% of your gross pay.</li>
        <li><strong>Social Security Tax (6.2%):</strong> Part of FICA (Federal Insurance Contributions Act). You pay 6.2% on all earnings up to the annual limit of $176,100 (in 2025). Your employer matches this with another 6.2%.</li>
        <li><strong>Medicare Tax (1.45%):</strong> Pays for healthcare coverage for Americans age 65+. Unlike Social Security, Medicare tax has no wage cap—it applies to every dollar you earn. If your income exceeds $200,000, you pay an additional 0.9% surtax.</li>
        <li><strong>State and Local Taxes (0% to 13.3%):</strong> Depending on where you live, state income tax can take another bite out of your paycheck. States like California, Hawaii, and New York have progressive rates up to 13.3%, while 9 states (Texas, Florida, Washington, Nevada, Tennessee, etc.) have 0% state income tax.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. Pre-Tax Deductions: Lower Taxes, Smaller Paychecks</h2>
      <p>Many workers forget that benefit elections made during HR onboarding reduce take-home pay immediately:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>401(k) / 403(b) Retirement:</strong> If you elected to save 6% of your salary to get an employer match, on a $75,000 salary, that is $173 deducted from every biweekly paycheck. While this lowers your taxable income, it reduces immediate cash in your pocket.</li>
        <li><strong>Health, Dental, & Vision Insurance:</strong> The employee share of health insurance premiums is deducted pre-tax each pay period (averaging $100 to $250 biweekly for single coverage, or $400+ for family plans).</li>
        <li><strong>HSA or FSA Contributions:</strong> Contributing to a Health Savings Account or Flexible Spending Account reduces gross pay before taxes are calculated.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Where Did $3,000 Go? A Real Biweekly Paycheck Breakdown</h2>
      <p>Here is what happens to a $75,000 annual salary ($2,885 gross every two weeks) for a single worker in a moderate-tax state like Illinois or Pennsylvania:</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Paystub Item</th><th>Amount</th><th>Percentage</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gross Pay</strong></td><td><strong>$2,885.00</strong></td><td>100.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>401(k) Contribution (6%)</td><td>-$173.10</td><td>6.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Health & Dental Insurance</td><td>-$125.00</td><td>4.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Taxable Wages</td><td>$2,586.90</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Federal Income Tax</td><td>-$262.40</td><td>9.1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Social Security Tax (6.2%)</td><td>-$178.87</td><td>6.2%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Medicare Tax (1.45%)</td><td>-$41.83</td><td>1.4%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>State Income Tax (~4.5%)</td><td>-$116.41</td><td>4.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Total Deductions</strong></td><td><strong>-$897.61</strong></td><td><strong>31.1%</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Net Pay (In Your Bank Account)</strong></td><td><strong>$1,987.39</strong></td><td><strong>68.9%</strong></td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>4. Why Is Your Paycheck Lower Than Your Co-Worker's?</h2>
      <p>Even if two workers make identical salaries at the same company, their net pay can differ significantly because of:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Form W-4 Setup:</strong> Marking Single vs. Married filing jointly drastically shifts how much the payroll software withholds. If you didn't claim children on Step 3, you are overwithholding.</li>
        <li><strong>Overtime and Supplemental Wages:</strong> Bonuses and overtime are often withheld at a flat supplemental rate of 22%, making that paycheck look disproportionately small.</li>
        <li><strong>Different Benefit Tiers:</strong> Choosing a PPO plan vs. an HDHP plan can swing paycheck deductions by hundreds of dollars per month.</li>
      </ol>

